WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:04.760
Have the government-mandated Coronavirus lockdowns
saved thousands of lives?

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:07.100
Or were they a terrible mistake?

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:12.110
That was the subject of an online Soho Forum
debate hosted June 1st, 2020.

00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:17.780
Defending the claim that the COVID-19 lockdowns
were the right move is Marty Makary, a surgical

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:21.820
oncologist at Johns Hopkins and author of
the 2019 book,

00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:26.520
"The Price We Pay, What Broke American Health Care-and How to Fix It."

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:32.439
Arguing the other side was epidimologist Knut 
Wittkowski, who's the CEO of ASDERA, the company

00:00:32.439 --> 00:00:38.500
discovering novel treatments for complex diseases
from data of genome-wide association studies.

00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.060
Wikowski also served for 20 years as head
of Department of Biostatistics, Epidemiology,

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:47.940
and Research Design at Rockefeller University
in New York.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.900
The Zoom conference was intermittently marred
by weak internet connection and audio lags,

00:00:52.900 --> 00:00:58.220
meaning that there were a few moments where Wittkowski wasn't audible that we had to edit out.

00:00:58.220 --> 00:01:04.360
But thankfully the recording is mostly in
tact, so here it is, Marty Makary and Knut Wittkowski

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.880
in an online debate moderated by Soho
Forum's director Gene Epstein.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:23.620
Tonight's resolution reads: the lockdown has saved hundreds of thousands, if not millions of lives,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:27.480
and spared American hospitals the horror of rationing care.

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:33.820
Speaking for the affirmative, Dr. Marty Makary a surgeon and professor of heath policy at the Johns Hopkins

00:01:33.820 --> 00:01:35.820
school of Public Health.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:46.520
Speaking for the negative, Dr. Knut Wittkowski, former head of biostatistics, epidimeology, and research design

00:01:46.520 --> 01:04:13.780
at Rockefeller University in New York.

01:04:15.000 --> 01:14:15.000
 

